[07:49] **How a call goes:**
[07:49] - **Opening:** a fixed first sentence says a digital assistant is calling from Freya Bank and the call is recorded. The agent then asks for the customer once, and a second time if the answer is unclear. Clear replies (wrong number, deceased, someone else answers) route straight to their ending without the model choosing.
[07:49] - **Someone else answers:** the agent only asks when the customer can be reached and books a callback inside the allowed window. That window is worked out in code from `now`.
[07:49] - **Identity:** date of birth first, then the last four phone digits. Each question has its own tool with the field fixed. The agent explains once why it asks; a second refusal closes as `not_verified` / `declined`. When the bank reports no wrong answers left, the call goes straight to `not_verified` / `failed`, and nothing else can win over it.
[07:49] - **Debt and promise:** after `get_account`, code works out the product name, floor, band, deadline, the attempt-1 day and a day-by-day calendar marking which days are allowed. The negotiation steps allow 2 refusals for the early band and 3 for the late band (the first ask plus 1 or 2 persuasion attempts). A refusal always moves forward, so a refused offer is never repeated, and running out of attempts ends as `no_promise` / `refused`.
[07:49] - **Endings:** each ending runs `close_call` alone and then hangs up. A recorded promise closes as `promise` over any other ending except a transfer, as the brief says.
[07:49] 
[07:49] **Decisions you may want to check:**
[07:49] - **Support transfer queue:** the brief says queue `support`, but the tool API only allows `customer_support`. The brief names the tool API as the reference for tool fields, so the transfer sends `customer_support`. The `close_call` reason is still `support`.
[07:49] - **Two products:** the brief says customers are late on one product, but it also says "one promise per product". The agent handles up to 2 products in case an account returns more.
[07:49] - **`get_account` failure:** the brief does not cover this. I close it as `no_promise` / `service_error`.
